[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced AI Architecture in Unreal Engine: Behavior Trees, Blackboards, and EQS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building intelligent, reactive non-player characters (NPCs) in Unreal Engine requires a robust architecture. The combination of Behavior Trees, Blackboards, and the Environmental Query System (EQS) forms the industry standard for creating complex AI logic that scales efficiently across different game genres."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding the Core Components"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before implementing the systems, it is vital to understand the role of each component within Unreal Engine's AI framework:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Blackboard: The centralized data storage (or memory) for the AI controller. It holds key-value pairs representing the AI state, such as target actors, last known locations, and health status."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Behavior Tree: The decision-making architecture that executes tasks sequentially or conditionally based on the data provided by the Blackboard."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Environmental Query System (EQS): A spatial query tool that allows the AI to analyze the world dynamically, finding optimal hiding spots, cover positions, or items to interact with."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Setting Up the Blackboard and Behavior Tree"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To begin, create a Blackboard Asset and a Behavior Tree Asset in your Content Browser. Right-click in your AI folder, navigate to Artificial Intelligence, and select 'Blackboard' and 'Behavior Tree'. Open your Blackboard asset and add a new Key named "TargetActor" of type Object, pointing to the Actor class."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, open the Behavior Tree asset and assign your newly created Blackboard in the details panel. Your Behavior Tree will now have access to the Blackboard keys during execution."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Implementing Custom Tasks"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Unreal Engine provides built-in tasks like MoveTo, you often need custom logic. Below is a conceptual example of a C++ BTTask node that checks if the AI has line of sight to the target."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"EBTNodeResult::Type UBTTask_CheckLineOfSight::ExecuteTask(UBehaviorTreeComponent& OwnerComp, uint8* NodeMemory)
{
    AAIController* AIController = OwnerComp.GetAIOwner();
    UBlackboardComponent* BlackboardComp = OwnerComp.GetBlackboardComponent();

    if (!AIController || !BlackboardComp) return EBTNodeResult::Failed;

    AActor* TargetActor = Cast<AActor>(BlackboardComp->GetValueAsObject("TargetActor"));
    APawn* AIPawn = AIController->GetPawn();

    if (TargetActor && AIPawn)
    {
        // Perform line of sight check logic here
        return EBTNodeResult::Succeeded;
    }

    return EBTNodeResult::Failed;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Integrating the Environmental Query System (EQS)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"EQS is used when the AI needs to make spatial decisions. For instance, finding the best cover position away from the player involves generating a grid of points, testing them against line of sight, and scoring them by distance."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generators: Create points in the world (e.g., Cone, Donut, or Grid around the AI)."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tests: Filter and score these points (e.g., Distance to Querier, Dot Product for Cover, Line of Sight to Target)."},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create an EQS Query asset, add a 'Points: Grid' generator, and attach a 'Distance' test set to scoring mode 'Distance to' with a filter to discard points too close to the threat."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Executing EQS from the Behavior Tree"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"To run your EQS query inside the Behavior Tree, add a custom task or use the built-in 'Run EQS Query' node. Store the resulting vector location back into a Blackboard Key (e.g., "BestCoverLocation"), and then pass that key into a standard 'MoveTo' task node."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"By structuring your AI this way, the Behavior Tree remains clean, data is safely decoupled in the Blackboard, and complex spatial reasoning is offloaded efficiently to EQS."}]
